id #edb/ref 17592186468294}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a963a554-e172-5944-b345-2fca4048d858" :question/math-academy-id "q-339709" :question/problem "![](images/78/78c4fafeaa4015c98d2a1817e47556957e00c87e265ca934c361a5f5941b6419.png)\n\nConsider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y)$ where the graph of $f(y)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $y=2$ is a node.\n- $y=-2$ is a sink.\n- $y=-2$ is a source.\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nThe phase line that corresponds to the given graph is as follows:\n\n![](images/90/9003cc9588b0838527f018463e2077d2d81bb5930ab70c1e3c713832e207bd99.png)\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n- Statement I is false. From the phase line, we see that $y=2$ is a sink.\n\n- Statement II is false, while statement III is true. From the phase line, we see that $y=-2$ is a source.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314832 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147495 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4535b48e-a33f-5f62-95d3-a5c0991c1f6d" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Equilibrium Points Using a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "273a38a2-c3a3-571b-a89b-cb8eb050565f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/r