3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467213}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5a5fa0b5-647d-500d-9ef8-ae8c45dc7293" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467213} :answer-field/id #uuid "bba3aa18-775b-5806-a452-96cfddbbd6dd" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468327}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8575f381-396c-5d27-97ba-96e6bcb9f145" :question/math-academy-id "q-342307" :question/problem "$$\n(x+2){y}^{″}+{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{(x+2)^{2}}y=0\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation given above. Which of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** The functions $P(x)=\\frac{1}{x+2}$ and $Q(x)=\\frac{1}{(x+2)^{3}}$ are analytic about $x=-2.$\n- **II.** The functions $(x+2)P(x)$ and $(x+2)^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=-2.$\n- **III.** The point $x=-2$ is an **irregular** singular point of the differential equation.\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "A singular point $a$ of the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=f(x)\n$$\n\nis regular if both $(x-a)P(x)$ and $(x-a)^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, the singular point is irregular.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the differential equation in standard form by dividing by $(x+2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+2){y}^{″}+{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{(x+2)^{2}}y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{x+2}{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{(x+2)^{3}}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{1}{x+2}\\,\\text{and}\\,Q(x)=\\frac{1}{(x+2)^{3}}.\n$$\n\nLet's now check each statement in turn.\n\n- Since the denominators of both $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ vanish at $x=-2,$ they are *not* analytic about $x=-2.$ So, statement I is false.\n\n- The function\n\n$$\n(x+2)P(x)=(x+2)\\frac{1}{x+2}=1\n$$\n\nis analytic about $x=-2,$ while the function\n\n$$\n(x+2)^{2}Q(x)=(x+2)^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{(x+2)^{3}}=\\frac{1}{x+2}\n$$\n\nis *not* analytic about $x=-2$ since its denominator vanishes at $x=-2.$ Therefore, statement II is false.\n\n- Since statement I is false, $x=-2$ is a singular point. Also, since statement II is false, it follows that $x=-2$ is an **irregular** singular point. Thus, statement III is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317246 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124677 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e7d1368-06ee-5135-b9f4-d3d4d2756b32" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Regular and Irregular Singular Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "718e262f-702d-56a3-8244-93f04bfef53f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467214} {:answer/id #uuid "d33c017a-0f5d-5aa8-ba9c-c5d244f3eff9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467215}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "718e262f-702d-56a3-8244-93f04bfef53f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467214} :answer-field/id #uuid "959c5bdb-6293-590c-9b69-fe8ed74b9410" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468328} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a01c891b-9640-5bf7-b431-f15b874e19b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467216} {:answer/id #uuid "aaf982c0-5ee0-5405-a471-2ec9c197a6bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467217}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a01c891b-9640-5bf7-b431-f15b874e19b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467216} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b373888-1863-5511-9d4b-535b06a0e035" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468329} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1396cfbb-e94b-5ff3-843f-4f53150668ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467218} {:answer/id #uuid "110b0e33-4690-56c6-b136-37d7f6a570d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467219}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "110b0e33-4690-56c6-b136-37d7f6a570d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467219} :answer-field/id #uuid "0df6a872-10ad-58fb-93ba-8af9a9bd7059" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468330}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2a6497aa-26dd-5916-a133-e72ffa5f99b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-342367" :question/problem "$$\n(x+2){y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{x+2}{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{(x+2)^{3}}y=0\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation given above. Which of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** The functions $P(x)=\\frac{1}{(x+2)^{2}}$ and $Q(x)=\\frac{1}{(x+2)^{4}}$ are analytic about $x=-2.$\n- **II.** The functions $(x+2)P(x)$ and $(x+2)^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=-2.$\n- **III.** The point $x=-2$ is an **irregular** singular point of the differential equation.\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "A singular point $a$ of the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=f(x)\n$$\n\nis regular if both $(x-a)P(x)$ and $(x-a)^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, the singular point is irregular.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the differential equation in standard form by dividing by $(x+2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+2){y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{x+2}{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{(x+2)^{3}}y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{(x+2)^{2}}{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{(x+2)^{4}}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{1}{(x+2)^{2}}\\,\\text{and}\\,Q(x)=\\frac{1}{(x+2)^{4}}.\n$$\n\nLet's now check each statement in turn.\n\n- Since the denominators of both $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ vanish at $x=-2,$ they are *not* analytic about $x=-2.$ So, statement I is false.\n\n- The function\n\n$$\n(x+2)P(x)=(x+2)\\cdot \\frac{1}{(x+2)^{2}}=\\frac{1}{x+2}\n$$\n\nis *not* analytic about $x=-2$ since its denominator vanishes at $x=-2.$ Therefore, statement II is false.\n\n- Since statement I is false, $x=-2$ is a singular point. Also, since statement II is false, it follows that $x=-2$ is an **irregular** singular point. Thus, statement III is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317247 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124677 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e7d1368-06ee-5135-b9f4-d3d4d2756b32" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Regular and Irregular Singular Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d08606d-59b8-595c-81c6-9ace551aafef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467220} {:answer/id #uuid "8a723a32-f03d-5352-add2-e9acfb9389cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467221}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4d08606d-59b8-595c-81c6-9ace551aafef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467220} :answer-field/id #uuid "662bc4f3-72c1-5097-a47a-4ad758f1d030" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468331} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b9636fc5-f28f-5b52-a351-4f2fe66aa496" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467222} {:answer/id #uuid "a6f3802a-30dd-5e97-a38e-5e7d92a60379" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467223}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b9636fc5-f28f-5b52-a351-4f2fe66aa496" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467222} :answer-field/id #uuid "4725cefe-7d99-55b4-ad4d-acb5823a39e7" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468332} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b8bbe69-c85e-5f85-bd63-af152c527d57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467224} {:answer/id #uuid "640f8725-f57b-500a-a639-2a062c60e52f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467225}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "640f8725-f57b-500a-a639-2a062c60e52f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467225} :answer-field/id #uuid "113903cb-1bc5-536f-9fd3-01031005c466" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468333}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6886daeb-f858-5dc5-8034-d2a1fce18371" :question/math-academy-id "q-342369" :question/problem "$$\n(x-4){y}^{″}+\\frac{2}{x-4}{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{(x-4)^{2}}y=0\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation given above. Which of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** The functions $P(x)=\\frac{2}{(x-4)^{2}}$ and $Q(x)=\\frac{1}{(x-4)^{3}}$ are analytic about $x=4.$\n- **II.** The functions $(x-4)P(x)$ and $(x-4)^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=4.$\n- **III.** The point $x=4$ is an **irregular** singular point of the differential equation.\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "A singular point $a$ of the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=f(x)\n$$\n\nis regular if both $(x-a)P(x)$ and $(x-a)^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, the singular point is irregular.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the differential equation in standard form by dividing by $(x-4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-4){y}^{″}+\\frac{2}{x-4}{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{(x-4)^{2}}y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{″}+\\frac{2}{(x-4)^{2}}{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{(x-4)^{3}}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{2}{(x-4)^{2}}\\,\\text{and}\\,Q(x)=\\frac{1}{(x-4)^{3}}.\n$$\n\nLet's now check each statement in turn.\n\n- Since the denominators of both $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ vanish at $x=4,$ they are *not* analytic about $x=4.$ So, statement I is false.\n\n- The function\n\n$$\n(x-4)P(x)=(x-4)\\cdot \\frac{2}{(x-4)^{2}}=\\frac{2}{x-4}\n$$\n\nis *not* analytic about $x=4,$ and the function\n\n$$\n(x-4)^{2}Q(x)=\\frac{1}{x-4}\n$$\n\nis also *not* analytic about $x=4$ since its denominator vanishes at $x=4.$ Therefore, statement II is false.\n\n- Since statement I is false, $x=4$ is a singular point. Also, since statement II is false, it follows that $x=4$ is an **irregular** singular point. Thus, statement III is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317270 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124677 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e7d1368-06ee-5135-b9